### What is a “Callback URL” (aka **Redirect URI**)?

When a user logs in with SoundCloud (OAuth flow), SoundCloud redirects the browser back to your site after the user authorizes your app.  
The **callback URL** is the exact address that SoundCloud will send the user to, and it must match one of the URLs you registered in your SoundCloud app settings.

---

## 1. Pick a URL on Your Site

| Scenario | Suggested URL | Why |
|----------|---------------|-----|
| **Production** | `https://yourdomain.com/auth/soundcloud/callback` | Clean, HTTPS, easy to remember. |
| **Staging / QA** | `https://staging.yourdomain.com/auth/soundcloud/callback` | Keeps dev and prod separate. |
| **Local Development** | `http://localhost:3000/auth/soundcloud/callback` | Works locally; SoundCloud allows `localhost` in the app settings. |
| **Single‑Page App** | `https://yourdomain.com/#/auth/soundcloud/callback` | If you’re using hash routing. |

> **Tip:** Keep the path consistent across environments (e.g., `/auth/soundcloud/callback`) and just change the domain.

---

## 2. Register the URL in SoundCloud

1. Go to <https://soundcloud.com/you/apps> and open your app.  
2. In the **“Redirect URI”** field, paste the URL you chose above.  
3. Click **Save** (or **Update**).  
4. **Important:** If you have multiple environments, you can add several redirect URIs separated by new lines.

---

## 3. Use the Callback URL in Your OAuth Flow

### 3.1 Build the Authorization URL

```js
const clientId     = 'YOUR_CLIENT_ID';
const redirectUri  = 'https://yourdomain.com/auth/soundcloud/callback';
const scope        = 'non-expiring';   // or whatever scopes you need
const state        = 'random_state_string'; // optional, for CSRF protection

const authUrl = `https://soundcloud.com/connect?client_id=${clientId}` +
                `&redirect_uri=${encodeURIComponent(redirectUri)}` +
                `&response_type=code` +
                `&scope=${encodeURIComponent(scope)}` +
                `&state=${encodeURIComponent(state)}`;

window.location.href = authUrl;   // redirect the user
```

### 3.2 Handle the Callback on Your Server

When SoundCloud redirects back, the URL will look like:

```
https://yourdomain.com/auth/soundcloud/callback?code=AUTH_CODE&state=random_state_string
```

Your server should:

1. Verify the `state` matches what you sent (protect against CSRF).  
2. Exchange the `code` for an access token.

```js
// Example (Node.js + Express)
app.get('/auth/soundcloud/callback', async (req, res) => {
  const { code, state } = req.query;

  // 1️⃣ Verify state
  if (state !== req.session.expectedState) {
    return res.status(400).send('Invalid state');
  }

  // 2️⃣ Exchange code for token
  const tokenResponse = await axios.post('https://api.soundcloud.com/oauth2/token', null, {
    params: {
      client_id: clientId,
      client_secret: clientSecret,
      redirect_uri: redirectUri,
      grant_type: 'authorization_code',
      code: code
    }
  });

  const accessToken = tokenResponse.data.access_token;
  // Store the token (e.g., in session or DB) and redirect the user
  req.session.accessToken = accessToken;
  res.redirect('/dashboard');
});
```

---

## 4. What If You Don’t Need OAuth?

If you’re only playing **public** tracks and don’t need to access a user’s private data, you can skip the OAuth flow entirely.  
In that case:

- **Leave the callback URL blank** (or use a placeholder like `https://yourdomain.com/`).
- Use the **Client ID** only in the SDK or embed URLs.

> **Caution:** Even if you skip OAuth, you still need to register a redirect URI in the SoundCloud app settings. SoundCloud requires at least one URI; you can set it to your site’s root or a dummy page.

---

## 5. Quick Checklist

| ✅ | Item |
|----|------|
| ✅ | Pick a clean, HTTPS URL for production. |
| ✅ | Register that URL (and any dev URLs) in the SoundCloud app. |
| ✅ | Use the same URL in your OAuth `redirect_uri` parameter. |
| ✅ | Verify the `state` parameter on callback. |
| ✅ | Exchange the `code` for an access token on your server. |
| ✅ | Store the token securely (session, DB, etc.). |

---

### Need a Concrete Example?

```html
<!-- index.html -->
<a href="/auth/soundcloud">Login with SoundCloud</a>
```

```js
// server.js (Express)
const express = require('express');
const axios = require('axios');
const session = require('express-session');
const app = express();

app.use(session({ secret: 'supersecret', resave: false, saveUninitialized: true }));

const clientId     = 'YOUR_CLIENT_ID';
const clientSecret = 'YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET';
const redirectUri  = 'https://yourdomain.com/auth/soundcloud/callback';

app.get('/auth/soundcloud', (req, res) => {
  const state = Math.random().toString(36).substring(2);
  req.session.expectedState = state;
  const authUrl = `https://soundcloud.com/connect?client_id=${clientId}` +
                  `&redirect_uri=${encodeURIComponent(redirectUri)}` +
                  `&response_type=code` +
                  `&scope=non-expiring` +
                  `&state=${state}`;
  res.redirect(authUrl);
});

app.get('/auth/soundcloud/callback', async (req, res) => {
  const { code, state } = req.query;
  if (state !== req.session.expectedState) return res.status(400).send('State mismatch');

  const tokenRes = await axios.post('https://api.soundcloud.com/oauth2/token', null, {
    params: {
      client_id: clientId,
      client_secret: clientSecret,
      redirect_uri: redirectUri,
      grant_type: 'authorization_code',
      code: code
    }
  });

  req.session.accessToken = tokenRes.data.access_token;
  res.redirect('/dashboard');
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('Listening on http://localhost:3000'));
```

That’s it! Once the user is authenticated, you can use the stored `accessToken` to make API calls or stream private tracks.

---

Let me know if you need help setting up the server code, handling the token, or anything else!

